==============================================================================
s04  Gating and the LSTM
      replacing a matrix product with an addition
==============================================================================

4.1  The idea, before the notation
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  S3's problem was a product. Travelling d steps back multiplied the
  gradient by d matrices, and a product of d similar factors is a
  geometric sequence -- it goes to zero, or it does not, and mostly it
  goes to zero.

  Addition has a derivative of 1. So: build a quantity that each step
  *adds to* rather than *transforms*, and its backward path stops
  shrinking. That quantity is the cell state, and the machinery around
  it exists to decide how much to add, how much to keep, and how much
  to reveal.
  eq:  c_t = f_t (*) c_{t-1} + i_t (*) g_t
       keep some of what you had, add some of what is new; (*) is elementwise multiplication
  eq:  h_t = o_t (*) tanh(c_t)
       the hidden state is a gated view of the cell state, not the cell state itself

4.2  What each gate is
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  symbol  name         what it decides                       typical value here
  ------  -----------  ------------------------------------  ------------------
  f_t     forget gate  how much of c_{t-1} survives          0.72
  i_t     input gate   how much of the candidate is written  0.5
  g_t     candidate    what would be written                 0.009
  o_t     output gate  how much of c_t is exposed as h_t     0.498
  hidden width d                     16
  parameters                         1344
  forget-gate bias at initialisation 1
  [PASS] every gate is a vector of values in (0, 1), decided per unit and per step
         the gates are outputs of a sigmoid reading h_{t-1} and x_t, so the network learns what to carry rather than being told
  # this is the part most summaries skip. The gates are not fixed decay constants; they are computed from the input at every step. An LSTM can hold one unit open for two hundred steps while cycling another every three, and which it does is learned

4.3  The derivative along the cell state
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  dc_t/dc_{t-1} = diag(f_t)
       holding h_{t-1} fixed: elementwise, and no weight matrix anywhere in it
  max |numeric - diag(f_t)|          4.562e-11
  largest off-diagonal entry         0.000e+00
  [PASS] the cell-state Jacobian is exactly the diagonal matrix of forget gates
         finite differences agree with diag(f_t) to 4.6e-11, and the off-diagonal entries are zero -- units do not mix along this path
  # compare with S3, where the same quantity was W^T diag(1 - s^2): a full matrix, applied once per step. Here it is a diagonal of numbers the network chose. Multiplying d diagonals together is still a product, but of quantities the network controls rather than of a fixed matrix's singular values

4.4  What that buys, measured against S3
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  distance back  vanilla RNN  LSTM cell path  GRU carry path
  -------------  -----------  --------------  --------------
             10     1.08e-05        3.39e-02        4.10e-02
             25     1.03e-12        2.79e-04        3.57e-04
             40     9.97e-20        2.20e-06        3.66e-06
             55     2.98e-27        1.81e-08        3.27e-08
  vanilla per-step ratio             0.3351
  LSTM highway per-step ratio        0.7225
  vanilla horizon (steps)            12.6
  LSTM highway horizon (steps)       42.5
  [PASS] the gated path decays enormously more slowly than the matrix path
         at distance 40 the vanilla gradient is 1.0e-19 of its starting size and the LSTM cell path is 2.2e-06
  [PASS] the GRU's update gate does the same job as the LSTM's forget gate
         GRU carry path at distance 40 is 3.7e-06, the same mechanism with one fewer gate and no separate cell state
  # the forget-gate bias is doing real work here. Initialised at 1.0 the gate starts near 0.73 and the product decays slowly; initialised at 0 it would start at 0.5 and the highway would halve the gradient every step, which is no better than the matrix it replaced. This is why that bias is a standard trick rather than a detail

4.5  Where 'the LSTM solves vanishing gradients' stops being true
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The cell state is not the only route back. h_{t-1} feeds all four
  gates, and h_{t-1} came from c_{t-1}, so there is a second path that
  does go through the weight matrix -- and behaves exactly like S3.
  distance back  vanilla RNN, full  LSTM, full  LSTM cell path (mechanism only)
  -------------  -----------------  ----------  -------------------------------
             10           1.08e-05    5.30e-02                         3.39e-02
             25           1.03e-12    3.63e-03                         2.79e-04
             40           9.97e-20    1.78e-04                         2.20e-06
             55           2.98e-27    7.38e-06                         1.81e-08
  [PASS] like for like, the LSTM's complete backward pass retains vastly more gradient than the vanilla network's
         at distance 40: LSTM 1.78e-04 against the RNN's 9.97e-20, both measured as complete backward passes
  [PASS] the two routes add rather than one attenuating the other
         the full pass (1.78e-04) exceeds the cell path alone (2.20e-06); gradient arriving by the matrix route is added to what the highway carries, not subtracted from it. The two are not directly comparable in any case -- the highway figure is a pure Jacobian product, the full figure a gradient with a source term -- and the honest comparison is the one above, against the ungated network
  # so the accurate statement is not that gating removes the vanishing gradient. The h-route still contains a matrix product and still behaves like S3; what changed is that a second route now exists which does not, and gradient arriving by it is added to whatever the matrix route delivers. Gating supplies a path that does not vanish; it does not repair the ones that do. That distinction matters because it predicts the failure mode: an LSTM whose forget gates learn to close will vanish again, and the gradient reaching a closed gate cannot reopen it

4.6  Two things gating does not touch
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  (a) c_t still cannot be computed before c_{t-1}. The sequential
      chain is exactly as long as it was.
  (b) The state is still a fixed-width vector. Everything the network
      knows about an arbitrarily long past is compressed into d
      numbers.
  state width d  bits available (fp32)  tokens distinguishable
  -------------  ---------------------  ----------------------
            256                  8,192                     525
            512                 16,384                   1,049
           1024                 32,768                   2,098
  [PASS] a fixed-width state cannot losslessly retain an arbitrarily long past, however good its gates
         distinguishing all |V|^T prefixes needs T log2|V| bits; d floats hold at most 32d, so d=512 bounds lossless recall near 1,049 tokens regardless of architecture
  # (a) is answered in S7 and (b) in S5 and S6. Note they are different kinds of problem: (a) is about wall-clock time and (b) is about information. Gating solved a third kind -- a gradient problem -- and solving one says nothing about the other two

4.7  What this made possible
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The functional consequence is worth stating plainly, because it is
  the reason the LSTM dominated sequence modelling for roughly two
  decades.

    before  a sequence model could reliably use about ten steps of
            context, so anything needing longer-range structure -- a
            pronoun and its antecedent, an opening balance and a
            closing one -- was out of reach

    after   speech recognition, handwriting recognition and machine
            translation all became practical on real inputs; sequence
            models over long event histories became worth building

  # and the next problem is already visible in 4.6(b). Once you ask a sequence model to *produce* a sequence rather than a label -- to translate a sentence rather than classify it -- that fixed-width state has to carry the entire input across to the output. S5 is about what that costs

s04 claims 7/7 passed in 0.05s
